  "narrative": {
    "intro": "RTX obligated $3.8B in federal contracts in FY2026 to date, across 673 awards and with an average award size of $5.6M. RTX obligations are down 48.3% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, accounting for $3.8B of obligations this fiscal year to date. RTX business concentrates in aerospace production and services: Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the largest market at $1.6B, followed by Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $806M and Aircraft Manufacturing at $805M. RTX also records $515M in Other Support Activities for Air Transportation and smaller amounts in research and missile manufacturing. Historical obligations show variation by year: FY21 was $3.7B, FY22 was $8B, FY23 was $8.2B, FY24 was $5B, and FY25 was $7.3B. Current-year totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date activity rather than a full-year total.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are RTX's federal obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounts for $3.8B of RTX obligations in FY2026 to date. RTX total obligations for FY2026 are $3.8B across 673 awards, with an average award size of $5.6M. RTX obligations are down 48.3% year over year, reflecting a smaller fiscal year to date total compared with prior full years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries drive RTX's federal revenue?",
        "body": "Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing drives $1.6B of RTX obligations in FY2026 to date. Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing and Aircraft Manufacturing follow at $806M and $805M respectively, with Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $515M and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $36.6M."
      }
    ]
  },
